.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt MGBA 6
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mgba
.Nd minimal Game Boy, Game Boy Color and Game Boy Advance player
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm mgba
.Op Fl -frames Ar N
.Op Fl -headless
.Ar ROM
.Nm mgba
.Fl -benchmark Ar N
.Op Fl -warmup Ar N
.Ar ROM
.Nm mgba
.Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs one uncompressed ROM through the native AM platform.
SDL2 provides video, queued audio, and keyboard-to-button mapping.
The emulator runs one frame at a time on the application thread.
No global configuration is read.
Built-in BIOS emulation is used.
The window opens at three times the native resolution and can be resized.
The picture retains its aspect ratio with nearest-neighbor scaling and black borders.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--headless"
.It Fl -frames Ar N
Run at most N frames without wall-clock pacing, fix emulated RTC time to
2000-01-01 UTC, and print a CRC32 of the final frame's packed RGB bytes.
.It Fl -headless
Disable the display and audio output. Requires
.Fl -frames .
.It Fl -benchmark Ar N
Measure N frames without display, audio output, pacing, or game input.
Software rendering and core audio generation remain enabled.
Print elapsed microseconds, frames per second, and the final frame CRC32.
Initialization, warmup, and checksum calculation are excluded from timing.
Cannot be combined with
.Fl -frames .
.It Fl -warmup Ar N
Run N frames before starting the benchmark timer (default 30; zero is valid).
Requires
.Fl -benchmark .
.El
.Sh CONTROLS
.Bl -tag -width Backspace
.It Arrow keys
Directional pad.
.It Z, X
A and B.
.It A, S
L and R on Game Boy Advance.
.It Enter
Start.
.It Backspace
Select.
.It Q, Escape
Quit. Closing the window also quits.
.El
All game keys are released when the window loses focus.
.Sh SAVES
Use the game's own save command.
Battery saves are written beside the ROM with its extension replaced by
.Pa .sav
and loaded automatically on the next run.
The ROM directory must be writable.
ROMs sharing the same basename in one directory share the same save file.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh AUTHORS
mGBA is developed by Jeffrey Pfau and contributors.
